{"record_id":"D49-B78-R003","dataset_id":"D49","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Statistical and Probabilistic Acceptance","subtopic":"Confidence-bound acceptance","concept":"When a requirement specifies a confidence bound, acceptance must use the declared confidence method and threshold rather than an informal estimate.","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","language":"English","language_version":"N/A","objective":"Ensure confidence-based acceptance decisions follow the specified statistical method.","requirements":["Confidence method is defined","Confidence level is defined","Bound or interval decision rule is defined"],"constraints":["No confidence level should be invented","Different statistical methods can produce different intervals"],"prerequisites":["Statistical requirement","Observed sample","Specified confidence method"],"dependencies":["D49-B78-R001","D49-B78-R002"],"input":{"metric":"failure_rate","upper_confidence_bound":0.01,"threshold":0.02,"confidence_level":0.95},"output":{"acceptance_status":"PASS"},"expected_behavior":"Acceptance passes when the declared confidence-bound condition is satisfied using the specified method.","correct_example":{"criterion":"PASS when the specified confidence bound satisfies the requirement at the declared confidence level."},"incorrect_example":{"criterion":"PASS because the point estimate is below the threshold."},"correction":"Evaluate the required confidence bound, not merely the point estimate.","failure_mode":"Uncertainty is ignored in acceptance decisions that explicitly require confidence bounds.","expected_error":"CONFIDENCE_BOUND_CRITERION_UNSATISFIED","security_implication":"Security-related probabilistic requirements can be overstated if uncertainty is ignored.","performance_implication":"Confidence intervals can expose uncertainty in performance measurements; no universal interval width is assumed.","testing_method":"Compute the required confidence bound using the declared method and compare it with the threshold.","test_input":{"sample":"observed_data","confidence_level":0.95},"expected_test_result":"Acceptance follows the declared bound criterion.","verification_method":"Independently recompute the confidence bound and compare it with the requirement.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":"structured_requirement_acceptance_methodology","evidence_level":"METHODOLOGY","confidence":"HIGH","relationships":[{"type":"requires","target":"D49-B78-R001"},{"type":"requires","target":"D49-B78-R002"},{"type":"verifies","target":"D49-B78-R003"}],"related_records":["D49-B78-R001","D49-B78-R002"]}

{"record_id":"D49-B78-R004","dataset_id":"D49","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","batch":"B78","topic":"Statistical and Probabilistic Acceptance","subtopic":"Threshold boundary acceptance","concept":"A statistical acceptance threshold must define how values exactly at, below, or above the boundary are classified.","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","language":"English","language_version":"N/A","objective":"Eliminate ambiguity at statistical acceptance boundaries.","requirements":["Threshold comparison operator is explicit","Boundary behavior is defined","Numeric precision policy is defined when relevant"],"constraints":["Do not assume inclusive or exclusive comparison","Rounding must not silently alter the decision"],"prerequisites":["Statistical metric","Threshold","Comparison rule"],"dependencies":["D49-B03-R002","D49-B78-R001"],"input":{"observed":0.02,"threshold":0.02,"operator":"<="},"output":{"acceptance_status":"PASS"},"expected_behavior":"Acceptance follows the explicitly declared comparison operator at the threshold boundary.","correct_example":{"criterion":"PASS when observed_value <= threshold if the requirement explicitly defines an inclusive upper bound."},"incorrect_example":{"criterion":"PASS at the threshold without specifying whether equality is allowed."},"correction":"Define the comparison operator and numeric handling explicitly.","failure_mode":"Equivalent measured values receive inconsistent acceptance decisions.","expected_error":"STATISTICAL_THRESHOLD_BOUNDARY_UNDEFINED","security_implication":"Boundary ambiguity can cause inconsistent security acceptance decisions.","performance_implication":"Latency or error-rate thresholds require explicit boundary and rounding semantics when used for acceptance.","testing_method":"Evaluate values below, equal to, and above the threshold.","test_input":{"values":[0.0199,0.02,0.0201]},"expected_test_result":"Each value receives the result dictated by the declared operator and precision policy.","ver