{"record_id":"B22-R001","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Data Integrity","concept":"Successful delivery requires explicit preservation of critical invariants across transformations","knowledge_type":"DATA_INTEGRITY_RULE","objective":"Capture the operational practice of defining and verifying invariants when data passes through multiple processing stages.","requirements":["Critical invariants must be explicitly identified","Transformations must preserve or intentionally change documented properties","Invalid states must be rejected or quarantined","Integrity evidence must be testable"],"constraints":["Not every field can remain unchanged across transformations","Integrity requirements depend on domain semantics"],"expected_behavior":"Each processing boundary preserves required invariants or produces an explicitly documented and validated state transition.","failure_mode":"Individual components appear correct while cumulative transformations silently corrupt or reinterpret important data.","root_cause":"Validation was performed locally at components without verifying end-to-end invariants.","security_implication":"Integrity failures can affect authorization, auditability, identity, or security decisions.","performance_implication":"Repeated validation can increase processing cost; critical invariants should be validated at appropriate trust boundaries.","testing_method":"Create end-to-end tests that establish input invariants, execute transformations, and assert required final invariants.","verification_method":"Trace each critical invariant across the processing pipeline and inspect evidence at every trust boundary.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established data-integrity and invariant-based engineering practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relatio