{"record_id":"B22-R002","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Configuration Management","concept":"Configuration must be reproducible and separated from uncontrolled runtime mutation","knowledge_type":"CONFIGURATION_PATTERN","objective":"Capture the operational pattern that successful systems make effective configuration identifiable, reproducible, and auditable.","requirements":["Configuration sources must be identifiable","Effective configuration must be reconstructable","Secrets must be handled separately from ordinary configuration","Runtime mutation must have explicit ownership and persistence semantics"],"constraints":["Environment-specific configuration is legitimate when explicitly modeled","Secret values must not be embedded in ordinary artifacts"],"expected_behavior":"Given the same approved configuration inputs and supported environment, the effective configuration can be reconstructed and compared.","failure_mode":"Two nominally identical deployments behave differently because configuration was inherited, mutated, or supplied through undocumented channels.","root_cause":"Configuration was treated as incidental environment state instead of a controlled input.","security_implication":"Undocumented configuration channels can bypass security review and expose credentials or weaken controls.","performance_implication":"Configuration differences can alter resource limits, concurrency, caching, or timeouts; performance evidence must identify the effective configuration.","testing_method":"Record configuration fingerprints or equivalent non-secret representations and compare them across environments.","verification_method":"Reconstruct effective configuration from approved sources and verify that prohibited or unexpected overrides are absent.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established configuration-management and reproducibility practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B11-B20"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D50:B21"},{"type":"tested_by","target":"D42"},{"type":"verified_by","target":"D43"}],"confidence":"HIGH"}

{"record_id":"B22-R003","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Dependency Management","concept":"Dependency reproducibility is part of project reproducibility","knowledge_type":"COMPATIBILITY_RULE","objective":"Capture the operational requirement that successful projects preserve the dependency set necessary to reproduce validated behavior.","requirements":["Direct dependencies must be identifiable","Resolved versions must be reconstructable","Transitive dependency changes must be detectable where relevant","Supported runtime versions must be recorded"],"constraints":["Exact dependency pinning is not universally required","Some environments intentionally permit controlled version ranges"],"expected_behavior":"The dependency environment used for validation can be reconstructed sufficiently to explain and reproduce supported behavior.","failure_mode":"A previously validated project behaves differently after dependency resolution changes even though application source code is unchanged.","root_cause":"Only direct dependency declarations were tracked while the resolved environment remained implicit.","security_implication":"Uncontrolled dependency changes can introduce vulnerable or compromised components.","performance_implication":"Dependency upgrades can change algorithmic behavior, resource consumption, or runtime performance; such changes require validation.","testing_method":"Build or provision the project from a clean environment using recorded dependency metadata and execute the relevant test suite.","verification_method":"Compare the reconstructed dependency environment with the environment associated with the validated artifact.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established reproducible-build and dependency-management practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B11-B20"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D50:B01-B10"},{"type":"tested_by","target":"D42"},{"type":"verified_by","target":"D43"}],"confidence":"HIGH"}

{"record_id":"B22-R004","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Backward Compatibility","concept":"Compatibility must be validated against actual supported consumers","knowledge_type":"COMPATIBILITY_RULE","objective":"Capture the operational lesson that preserving an interface shape is insufficient when semantic compatibility is required.","requirements":["Supported consumers must be identified","Compatibility expectations must be explicit","Representative old-version inputs must be tested","Breaking changes must be versioned or deliberately migrated"],"constraints":["Compatibility cannot be guaranteed for unsupported consumers","Semantic changes can be breaking even when schemas remain structurally compatible"],"expected_behavior":"Supported existing consumers continue to operate according to their documented expectations after a compatible change.","failure_mode":"A change passes local tests but breaks an existing consumer because behavior, defaults, timing, errors, or semantics changed.","root_cause":"Compatibility was evaluated structurally rather than behaviorally.","testing_method":"Run regression and compatibility tests using representative supported consumer behavior and boundary cases.","verification_method":"Compare observed behavior against the compatibility contract for every supported interface version.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established backward-compatibility and regression-engineering practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B21"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D46"},{"type":"tested_by","target":"D42"},{"type":"verified_by","target":"D43"}],"confidence":"HIGH"}

{"record_id":"B22-R005","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Release Validation","concept":"Release success requires evidence for the exact artifact being delivered","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","objective":"Capture the rule that validation evidence must correspond to the exact release artifact and configuration intended for deployment.","requirements":["The release artifact must be uniquely identifiable","Validation must reference the released artifact","Relevant configuration and dependency state must be recorded","Promotion must not silently substitute an unvalidated artifact"],"constraints":["Build pipelines may produce multiple artifacts","Artifact identity must remain stable across promotion stages"],"expected_behavior":"The artifact promoted to an operational environment is demonstrably the same artifact, or a deterministically equivalent artifact, that passed required validation.","failure_mode":"Tests pass against one build while deployment uses another build or altered configuration.","root_cause":"Artifact identity was not included in the validation-to-deployment chain.","security_implication":"Artifact substitution can bypass security validation and integrity controls.","performance_implication":"A different build or dependency resolution can invalidate performance measurements and capacity assumptions.","testing_method":"Record artifact identity and verify it at test, packaging, promotion, and deployment boundaries.","verification_method":"Reconcile release identifiers, artifact hashes or equivalent identity, configuration references, and validation evidence.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established release-integrity, CI/CD, and verification practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B11-B20"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D49"},{"type":"verified_by","target":"D43"}],"confidence":"HIGH"}

{"record_id":"B23-R001","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Decision Evidence","concept":"Important engineering decisions should retain the evidence available when the decision was made","knowledge_type":"DECISION_PATTERN","objective":"Capture decision provenance so later operators can distinguish evidence-based decisions from assumptions and reconstruct why a successful project took its final form.","requirements":["Decision context must be recorded","Alternatives considered must be identifiable","Available evidence must be linked","Decision consequences must be observable where possible"],"constraints":["Historical decisions may have incomplete evidence","Later information must not be retroactively represented as if it were available at decision time"],"expected_behavior":"A reviewer can reconstruct the decision context and distinguish known facts, assumptions, constraints, and subsequent outcomes.","failure_mode":"A successful outcome causes retrospective reasoning to incorrectly treat the original decision as obviously correct.","root_cause":"Decision records captured conclusions but not the evidence and uncertainty present at decision time.","testing_method":"Audit decision records for separation of evidence, assumptions, constraints, and outcomes.","verification_method":"Check that decision provenance predates or is contemporaneous with the decision and that later outcome evidence is stored separately.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established decision-record and engineering provenance practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B01-B10"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D54"},{"type":"derived_from","target":"D50"}],"confidence":"HIGH"}

{"record_id":"B23-R002","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Assumption Management","concept":"Explicit assumptions prevent accidental promotion of uncertainty into requirements","knowledge_type":"TRACEABILITY_RULE","objective":"Capture the distinction between verified requirements and assumptions that remain subject to validation.","requirements":["Assumptions must be explicitly labeled","Assumptions affecting architecture or acceptance must have validation plans","Validated assumptions must be converted into evidence rather than silently remaining assumptions","Invalidated assumptions must trigger impact analysis"],"constraints":["Some assumptions are unavoidable during early project stages","An assumption is not evidence merely because implementation depends on it"],"expected_behavior":"Project reasoning distinguishes requirements, facts, assumptions, hypotheses, and verified outcomes.","failure_mode":"An unverified assumption becomes embedded in implementation and later appears to be a documented requirement.","root_cause":"Project records lacked explicit epistemic status.","testing_method":"Review project records for assumptions affecting scope, architecture, dependencies, security, performance, and acceptance.","verification_method":"Trace high-impact assumptions to validation evidence or explicit unresolved status.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established requirements engineering and evidence-traceability practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B01-B10"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D49"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D58"}],"confidence":"HIGH"}

