{"record_id":"B23-R004","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Outcome Attribution","concept":"Outcome claims must distinguish project effects from unrelated environmental changes","knowledge_type":"ANALYSIS_RULE","objective":"Capture the operational lesson that successful outcomes should not automatically be attributed entirely to the delivered change.","requirements":["Outcome measurement context must be recorded","Relevant environmental changes must be identified","Baseline and post-change observations should be distinguished","Attribution uncertainty must be preserved"],"constraints":["Controlled experiments are not always possible in production systems","External factors may be only partially observable"],"expected_behavior":"Outcome records distinguish observed change from the degree of confidence that the project caused that change.","failure_mode":"A favorable metric after deployment is treated as proof that the project alone produced the improvement.","root_cause":"Temporal correlation was treated as causal evidence.","security_implication":"Security improvements should not be attributed to a change without considering concurrent control changes and threat-environment changes.","performance_implication":"Performance changes require awareness of workload, infrastructure, configuration, and dependency changes.","testing_method":"Compare baseline and post-change measurements while recording material concurrent changes.","verification_method":"Review whether causal attribution is explicitly supported, qualified, or marked uncertain.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established experimental and operational measurement practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B01-B10"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D56"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D58"}],"confidence":"HIGH"}

{"record_id":"B23-R005","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Learning Eligibility","concept":"Only sufficiently verified project outcomes should become reusable engineering knowledge","knowledge_type":"LEARNING_RULE","objective":"Prevent the knowledge fabric from learning generalized rules from unverified anecdotes or weakly attributed project outcomes.","requirements":["Source project evidence must be identifiable","Outcome verification status must be known","Generalization must preserve scope and limitations","Contradictory evidence must remain discoverable"],"constraints":["A successful project can contain individual unverified claims","A single project experience should not automatically become a universal rule"],"expected_behavior":"Reusable knowledge is derived only from evidence whose provenance, validation state, scope, and limitations are explicit.","failure_mode":"An anecdotal success is generalized into a rule and later causes inappropriate engineering decisions.","root_cause":"Learning eligibility was based on narrative confidence rather than evidence quality.","testing_method":"Attempt to ingest project lessons with varying validation states and verify that only eligible records enter the reusable-knowledge layer.","verification_method":"Check provenance, validation status, scope, contradictions, and supporting evidence before promoting a lesson.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Evidence-based learning and engineering knowledge-management practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B01-B10"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D58"},{"type":"requires","target":"D50"}],"confidence":"HIGH"}

{"record_id":"B24-R001","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Failure Containment","concept":"Successful systems constrain failure propagation at explicit boundaries","knowledge_type":"RESILIENCE_PATTERN","objective":"Capture the operational pattern of preventing a local component failure from silently corrupting unrelated project functionality.","requirements":["Failure domains must be identifiable","Boundary behavior must be explicit","Partial failure must not masquerade as success","Critical shared resources require protection"],"constraints":["Isolation mechanisms introduce complexity and resource overhead","Some dependencies necessarily create coupled failure domains"],"expected_behavior":"A failure within a bounded component produces a controlled, observable impact rather than silent propagation.","failure_mode":"A local fault propagates through shared state, retries, queues, or dependencies and causes broader corruption or outage.","root_cause":"Failure boundaries were implicit or absent.","security_implication":"Failure propagation can cross trust boundaries and create unauthorized or unsafe state transitions.","performance_implication":"Isolation, backpressure, retries, and circuit mechanisms affect latency and throughput and should be measured when material.","testing_method":"Inject representative dependency and component failures and observe affected versus unaffected domains.","verification_method":"Confirm that documented failure boundaries correspond to actual observed system behavior.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established fault-isolation and resilience engineering practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B21"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D44"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D45"}],"con