{"record_id":"B24-R002","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Idempotency","concept":"Repeatable operations reduce recovery ambiguity when retries are possible","knowledge_type":"IMPLEMENTATION_PATTERN","objective":"Capture the successful operational use of idempotent behavior for operations that may be retried because of timeout, partial failure, or uncertain completion.","requirements":["Retryable operations must define duplicate-request behavior","An operation identity or equivalent deduplication mechanism must be available where needed","Side effects must have explicit commit semantics","Ambiguous completion must be recoverable"],"constraints":["Not every operation can be made idempotent","Idempotency keys require appropriate lifecycle and storage semantics"],"expected_behavior":"Repeating an operation with the same logical request does not create unintended duplicate side effects.","failure_mode":"A client retries after an uncertain response and the operation is applied twice.","root_cause":"The operation contract assumed one request maps to one execution attempt.","security_implication":"Duplicate side effects can create repeated privileged actions or inconsistent authorization state.","performance_implication":"Idempotency tracking adds storage and lookup overhead; its cost must be evaluated for high-throughput paths.","testing_method":"Execute the same logical request repeatedly, including retries after simulated timeouts or partial completion.","expected_test_result":"The final state reflects the defined idempotency contract without unintended duplicate side effects.","verification_method":"Inspect operation identity, persistence, and final state across repeated execution scenarios.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established distributed-systems retry and idempotency practice","ev