{"record_id":"B24-R005","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"A successful project explicitly defines reduced-capability behavior for non-critical dependency failure","knowledge_type":"RESILIENCE_PATTERN","objective":"Capture the operational practice of maintaining safe, useful behavior when selected non-critical capabilities are unavailable.","requirements":["Critical and non-critical capabilities must be distinguished","Degraded behavior must be explicitly defined","Users or operators must be able to identify degraded state","Security and data-integrity guarantees must remain intact"],"constraints":["Degradation must not silently weaken critical guarantees","Some dependencies have no safe degraded mode"],"expected_behavior":"When a non-critical dependency fails, the system enters a documented reduced-capability state while preserving required safety and integrity properties.","failure_mode":"The system either fails unnecessarily for a non-critical dependency or silently continues with incorrect assumptions.","root_cause":"Dependency criticality and degraded modes were not modeled.","security_implication":"Fallback behavior can accidentally bypass security controls if the primary security mechanism is treated as optional.","performance_implication":"Fallback paths may have different latency or resource characteristics and should be measured when operationally important.","testing_method":"Disable selected non-critical dependencies and verify documented degraded behavior and preserved invariants.","verification_method":"Confirm degraded-state indicators, capability restrictions, and security/data-integrity guarantees.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established resilience and graceful-degradation engineering practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE