{"record_id":"B25-R002","dataset_id":"D50","dataset_version":"1.0","schema_version":"1.0","topic":"Successful Projects","subtopic":"Least Privilege","concept":"Successful operational delivery limits permissions to those required by defined responsibilities","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","objective":"Capture the practice of validating that service and human permissions are no broader than required for supported operations.","requirements":["Required permissions must be identified","Unused privileges must be removable","Privileged operations must have explicit ownership","Permission changes must be auditable"],"constraints":["Least privilege must account for legitimate operational recovery","Overly restrictive permissions can prevent required functionality and create unsafe workarounds"],"expected_behavior":"Components and operators can perform authorized tasks without possessing unnecessary capabilities.","failure_mode":"Broad permissions are granted to avoid integration or deployment friction and remain after delivery.","root_cause":"Permission design followed convenience rather than explicit capability requirements.","testing_method":"Attempt required operations under the minimum intended permissions and attempt representative unauthorized operations.","verification_method":"Compare effective permissions with documented capability requirements and inspect privileged paths.","validation_status":"GENERATED","execution_status":"NOT_EXECUTED","provenance":{"type":"validated_operational_engineering_practice","basis":"Established least-privilege security engineering practice","evidence_status":"GENERALIZED_PRACTICE_NOT_PROJECT_SPECIFIC"},"relationships":[{"type":"extends","target":"D50:B25-R001"},{"type":"related_to","target":"D47"},{"type":"tested_by","target":"D42"}],"confidence":"HIGH"}

